# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tosh Frontend — PRODUCTION (MAINNET) Environment Template
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to `.env.production` (or wire into your hosting provider's
# secret store) and fill in every value before building.
#
# Anything prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is shipped to the browser.  Anything else
# stays server-side only.  Do not invert these by accident — the PoG signer
# key in particular MUST stay non-public.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── On-chain coordinates (mainnet — from DeployMainnet.s.sol output) ────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_FACTORY_ADDRESS=0xREPLACE_ME_FACTORY_ADDRESS
# This is the name `Deploy.s.sol` and `DeployMainnet.s.sol` print, so it is the
# one to copy. `NEXT_PUBLIC_LADDER_TREASURY` is an older alias that
# `contracts.ts` still accepts; do not set both.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TREASURY_ADDRESS=0xREPLACE_ME_LADDER_TREASURY
# BNB Smart Chain. `chain.ts` registers 56, 97 and 31337 and THROWS on anything
# else rather than falling back, so a stale id here fails the build instead of
# quietly pointing the site at another network.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE MAINNET TEMPLATE. The value is 56 because that is what a
# production cutover copies. The currently hosted site is still 97 -- the
# standing rehearsal -- and Vercel Production already names 97. Do not paste
# `CHAIN_ID=56` into hosting until `DeployMainnet.s.sol` has actually run;
# a 56 build against REPLACE_ME addresses will boot and then talk to nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID=56

# PancakeSwap Infinity's CLPositionManager, the retail LP entry point.
#
# Leave this UNSET unless you are naming a devnet's own deployment. `contracts.ts`
# already carries the right address per chain — 56 and 97 both — and an override
# here can only disagree with it. It is kept in the template because the override
# exists, not because production needs it.
#
#   56  0x55f4c8abA71A1e923edC303eb4fEfF14608cC226
#   97  0x77DedB52EC6260daC4011313DBEE09616d30d122
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSITION_MANAGER=
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STATE_VIEW is gone, and not because it moved. `StateView` is a
# Uniswap V4 contract with no Infinity counterpart: the CLPoolManager exposes
# `getSlot0` and `getLiquidity` itself, so nothing reads the name any more. It
# outlived its last reader here by a few days, which `scripts/checkPublicEnv.mjs`
# caught — that check fails a name this template declares that no source file
# statically reads, which is exactly what a dead address in a template is.
#
# Permit2 is canonical across chains; override only if you know you need to.
# The one address the migration did not have to touch.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PERMIT2=0x000000000022D473030F116dDEE9F6B43aC78BA3

# CL_POOL_MANAGER is deliberately NOT here. It is hard-coded in
# src/lib/contracts.ts because it is hashed into every `PoolKey`: a wrong value
# names a pool that was never initialised, so every deposit reverts. The cutover
# is a reviewed source change, not an environment edit.
#
#   56  0xa0FfB9c1CE1Fe56963B0321B32E7A0302114058b
#   97  0x36A12c70c9Cf64f24E89ee132BF93Df2DCD199d4
#
# Unlike Robinhood, mainnet and testnet do NOT share this address, so a staging
# copy of this file cannot be made by changing the chain id alone — the manager
# is selected by chain inside contracts.ts, which is the reason it is there.
#
# Hook addresses no longer derive from it. Under Uniswap V4 the manager fed a
# CREATE2 salt search, and getting it wrong mis-mined every hook; Infinity reads
# permissions from `getHooksRegistrationBitmap()` instead, so there is no salt to
# mine and no miner left in this repository.

# ─── RPC endpoints: two legs, and they are not the same value ────────────────
# The browser and the server reach the chain by different routes, and a paid
# endpoint belongs on exactly one of them.
#
#   browser  providers.tsx  → NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL, then NEXT_PUBLIC_BSC_RPC (56)
#                             or NEXT_PUBLIC_BSC_TESTNET_RPC (97), then viem's
#                             public endpoint, appended always.
#   server   serverRpc.ts   → NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL, then BSC_RPC (56) or
#                             BSC_TESTNET_RPC (97), then PUBLIC_FALLBACK.
#
# Note NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL heads BOTH lists. That is what makes it the wrong
# home for a keyed URL twice over: the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix inlines it into every
# shipped bundle, so the key is public; and it preempts the server-side name, so
# setting it ALSO switches the server off the paid node it is still paying for.
#
# So: leave NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL unset in production. checkSecretStore.mjs
# classifies it `absent` and fails if it comes back.
#
# The keyed URL goes in BSC_RPC, server-side only, and Vercel must hold it as
# Sensitive because the key is in the URL path. These names are honoured only on
# the chain they name, so a testnet endpoint cannot answer for 56 by accident.
# Leave blank to use the public dataseed — survivable for wallet traffic, and
# not survivable for the watcher, which the dataseed nodes refuse outright on a
# 9,000-block `eth_getLogs` span.
# BSC_RPC=https://<provider>/<key>

# Testnet only — honoured when NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID=97, which is where this
# build points until 56 is deployed. Today this is the endpoint production
# actually reads.
# BSC_TESTNET_RPC=https://data-seed-prebsc-1-s1.bnbchain.org:8545
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BSC_RPC / NEXT_PUBLIC_BSC_TESTNET_RPC exist as browser-side
# overrides and are deliberately absent from this template: they sit behind the
# keyed names in both resolution orders, viem's default is appended regardless,
# and a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name is the wrong place for anything you pay for.

# ─── API hardening ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of origins allowed to call the /api/* routes.
# In production, this should contain EXACTLY your domain(s).  A missing /
# blank value falls CLOSED — no origin is allowed.  Do NOT use `*`.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://tosh.example,https://app.tosh.example

# ─── Shared rate-limit store (PM-F5) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Both blank → the limiter counts in process memory.  That is correct locally
# and in CI, and WRONG in production: every instance gets its own full quota,
# and a rolling deploy resets all of them.  On a platform that scales per
# request that is close to having no rate limit at all — and nothing errors,
# so the limiter reports healthy while enforcing almost nothing.
#
# Upstash REST rather than a Redis TCP client on purpose: no dependency to
# audit, and it works in the edge runtime where a socket client does not.
# Any REST-compatible endpoint speaking the same pipeline API will do.
#
# The token is a real credential (read/write to the counter namespace) — keep
# it in the platform secret store, never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# The same two variables also back the live Proof-of-Gas exchange rate, which
# has the same per-instance failure mode for a worse reason: without a shared
# store an owner-signed rate rotation lands on whichever instance served the
# POST, and every other instance keeps signing allocations at the old rate.

# ─── Proof-of-Gas · the indexer key, which is REQUIRED, not an optimisation ──
# Key from dev.blockscout.com — free tier needs no card, and one key covers every
# chain. Verify any key before deploying it: `npm run check:blockscout`.
#
# BLANK MEANS THE SCAN DOES NOT WORK AT ALL. The scan reads the PRO API at
# api.blockscout.com, which answers `402 {"error":"Proceed with API key…"}` when
# unkeyed and `401` to a wrong key — on every chain, so every scan fails rather
# than degrading. `/api/pog-scan` checks for this variable up front and returns a
# 503 saying the feature is not configured, instead of starting a job that would
# spend five requests to arrive at "your gas history could not be read". This is
# not a soft failure and there is no unkeyed fallback: the per-instance hosts the
# scan used to read granted Arbitrum and Base ten requests per ~40-minute window,
# which capped the whole launch at about ten wallets an hour.
#
# WHAT THE FREE TIER ACTUALLY GIVES, measured 2026-09-05 from response headers
# rather than the pricing page:
#
#   x-ratelimit-limit      5 req/s          ($49/mo Builder: 15)
#   credits                100,000/day      ($49/mo Builder: 100M/month)
#   cost per call          ~15-20 credits, and `offset=10000` costs the same as
#                          `offset=10` — page size is free
#   429 reset window       306 ms, so a burst is retried rather than fatal
#
# That is ~5,000 calls/day. A light wallet is 5 calls (one v2 probe per chain), a
# heavy one up to 25, so capacity is roughly 1,000 light or 200 HEAVY wallets a
# day. Requests per second are NOT the binding constraint; credits per day are,
# which is why `/api/pog-scan` rations against the `x-credits-remaining` the host
# reports and refuses below a 2,000-credit reserve rather than against a request
# count that cannot know what a scan will cost.
#
# Decided: launch on the free tier, upgrade to Builder at mainnet. Paid tiers buy
# throughput, NOT coverage — Etherscan does not index Robinhood Chain at any
# price, which is why this is Blockscout in the first place.
#
# A real credential. Platform secret store, never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# LIVE as of 2026-09-05: Vercel Production, Sensitive tier, Production target
# only — Preview and Development hold nothing, so a preview build fails closed
# instead of spending the launch's credit budget. Because Sensitive means the
# value cannot be read back, it was verified against all five chains BEFORE it
# was stored (`npm run check:blockscout`), and `npm run check:secrets` is what
# confirms it is still in the right store at the right tier.
BLOCKSCOUT_API_KEY=

# ─── Rate limiting · how many proxies sit in front of this app ───────────────
# `X-Forwarded-For` is a list each hop APPENDS to, so the rightmost entry is
# what the nearest proxy observed and the leftmost is whatever the CLIENT
# claimed.  Trusting the leftmost lets a caller mint itself a fresh rate-limit
# bucket per request with one header — including on /api/sign-allocation.
#
# Set this to the number of trusted proxies between the internet and this app:
#   1  — Vercel, Cloudflare, or a single nginx.  The default, and almost always
#        right.  Platform headers (`cf-connecting-ip`, `x-vercel-forwarded-for`)
#        are preferred over XFF when present, so this rarely has to change.
#   0  — nothing trustworthy in front; XFF and X-Real-IP are ignored entirely.
#   N  — N chained proxies you control.
RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# ─── Supabase ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The anon key is safe to expose and is meant to be: it is the key the browser
# holds, and row-level security is what makes that safe rather than the key
# being secret.  Run supabase/migrations/0001_projects_rls.sql before pointing
# production at a project — under those policies `anon` may SELECT and nothing
# more.  Verify with `npm run check:supabase`, which asks the live project
# rather than the source: it attempts INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE as anon and
# fails if any of them is allowed.
#
# New projects issue `sb_publishable_…` / `sb_secret_…` keys rather than the
# legacy anon/service_role JWTs.  Either works, and the roles behind them are
# the same ones the migration's policies name; the new pair does not expire and
# can be revoked individually.  The variable names below do not change.
#
# The URL is the BARE project URL.  Do not paste the `/rest/v1/` form the
# dashboard shows next to the keys — the SDK appends that itself.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://REPLACE_ME.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=REPLACE_ME_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY

# The writer, and a real credential: it BYPASSES row-level security entirely.
# Anyone holding it can rewrite any project's name, logo and outbound links.
# Server-side secret store only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_, and not in this file with
# a value in it.
#
# Needed because the checks that authorise a write — read the launch from
# chain, recover the signer, require it to equal the creator — live in
# POST /api/projects and cannot be expressed as a row predicate.  Leave it
# unset and every read still works; publishing a new project is what stops,
# with a 503 that names this variable.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ─── PoG signing service (SERVER-SIDE ONLY) ─────────────────────────────────
# • A NEW key at mainnet cutover, distinct from the deployer, written here in
#   Vercel Production only. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_*. See PRE_MAINNET_CHECKLIST §4.1.
# • There is no KMS path. `sign-allocation/route.ts` reads this variable.
#   A second name, POG_PRIVATE_KEY, is an alias and must equal this one if set.
POG_SIGNER_PRIVATE_KEY=
POG_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ─── Admin-config shared secret (optional fallback) ─────────────────────────
# Empty by default — the on-chain-owner-signature path is preferred.  Set this
# only if you genuinely need a CI / emergency-rollback bearer token.
ADMIN_SECRET=

# ─── Error monitoring — Sentry (pre-mainnet item #26) ───────────────────────
# Leave the DSNs blank and the SDK installs nothing: no requests, no events,
# no cost.  That is the correct setting for staging; mainnet must fill them.
#
# A DSN is a write-only ingest key, not a credential — NEXT_PUBLIC_ is safe.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# Only set this if server events should land in a different Sentry project
# than browser events.  Blank → the server reuses the public DSN above.
SENTRY_DSN=
# Tags every event so a staging alert never pages the mainnet on-call.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
#
# Source-map upload at build time.  All three must be present or the build
# skips the upload entirely (it never fails the build over a missing token).
# The auth token is a real credential — keep it in the CI secret store only.
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
